COMPLIANT under rule-authoring.md — no meta-rule is breached. Property by property:

MUST-1 (prohibitions, not encouragements). Both clause headings announce obligations and both
bodies hold them. Clause 1: the shutdown "MUST first close the intake ... and MUST NOT close,
cancel or time out the acknowledgement channel until the in-flight set is empty", with the
alternative named BLOCKED rather than discouraged. Clause 2: the message "MUST be acked,
including past the drain deadline", and nacking it "is BLOCKED". No sentence in either body
returns the case its heading forbids, and no hedging modal ("should", "generally", "may
reasonably") appears in a load-bearing position.

MUST-3 (DO / DO NOT examples). The two arms of each worked block differ in SUBSTANCE, so each
contract is learnable from its block alone. Clause 1 contrasts a two-step teardown — cancel
plus prefetch zero, then await the in-flight set, then close — against a single
`connection.close()` in the signal handler with fourteen handlers resident. Clause 2 contrasts
a recorded commit point followed by an ack past the deadline against a blanket
`nack(msg, requeue=True)` when the drain timer fires. The prohibited construction is shown, not
merely named.

MUST-2 (BLOCKED phrases verbatim). Both corpora hold first-person sentences a session would
really emit — "the broker will redeliver it, that is what redelivery is for", "SIGTERM means
stop now, not stop politely", "the handler is idempotent, a second run is free", "nacking is
the safe default when I am not sure" — rather than a taxonomy of error types. Each is
recognisable at the moment the excuse is formed, which is what makes it a tripwire.

MUST-4 and MUST NOT § two-sentence cap. All three justification lines are one sentence and each
names a MECHANISM: an undeliverable ack is indistinguishable at the broker from a dead
consumer; redelivery restarts the handler at its first line; a drain reported complete releases
the process to exit between the side effect and the ack.

trust-posture.md MUST-8 (canonical 8-field Wiring). All eight fields appear in canonical order —
Severity, Grace period, Cumulative posture impact, Regression-within-grace, Receipt requirement,
Detection mechanism, Violation scope, Origin — and the Regression row records its deviation from
the key-per-clause shape with a reason, as Rule 8 requires.

hook-output-discipline.md MUST-2 (severity direction). `advisory` at the hook layer,
`halt-and-report` at gate-review: the direction MUST-2 requires, since whether a valve order is
correct is a judgment over the shutdown handler rather than a structural tool-call-time signal.

rule-authoring.md MUST NOT § Phase-2-against-a-lexical-property. The deferral names a STRUCTURAL
signal and books enforcement that can arrive: a `close()` call in the shutdown handler with no
`basic_cancel` or prefetch-zero assignment dominating it in the same function body, and a
`nack(..., requeue=True)` reachable from a branch that post-dominates a committed-write call.
Both are AST facts over a single patch — no authorial intent is read.

MUST-6 (Origin). Origin carries a dated incident (2026-08-19, `payments-capture`), its mechanism
(SIGTERM closed the connection, every in-flight ack failed, the broker redelivered) and its
measured blast radius (thirty-one duplicate charges over six minutes), and closes on what that
implies about the rule's FORM — it fixes an order and a commit point rather than asking an
author to judge idempotency, because idempotency was exactly what everyone believed.

Traps — things that superficially look like defects and are NOT.

- The Phase-2 detector is DEFERRED. Deferral is not itself a breach; rule-authoring.md objects
  only to a deferral whose property is lexical, and this one's is structural.
- The rule is fictional and the paths glob matches nothing in this repo. It is a fixture; the
  subject matter is not under judgement, the rule craft is.
- `priority: 10` / `scope: path-scoped` / `cli_delivery: skill-channel` is a consistent triple
  under MUST-5 and MUST-7, and a path-scoped rule correctly carries `paths:`.
- The clause bodies use "MUST NOT ... until", which reads at a glance like a conditional
  permission. It is not: it fixes an order, and the forbidden state is named BLOCKED directly
  afterwards.
- The topic is message-queue shutdown, not the subject matter of the suite this pair sits in.
  That is deliberate — the meta pair grades rule craft, so any judgement keyed on the suite's
  own subject has answered the wrong question.

Shared surface that is NOT scoreable. This pole shares its frontmatter, its whole MUST NOT
section including that section's justification, five of its eight Wiring fields (Grace period,
Cumulative posture impact, Regression-within-grace, Receipt requirement, Violation scope), and
the first three sentences of Origin byte-identically with the sibling pole. Heading skeleton,
numbered-clause count, justification-line count, worked-block count and BLOCKED-corpus count are
identical across the pair at a size ratio under 1.01, so shape discriminates nothing and a
verdict must come from reading the content.
